From: HarmonyOS PC adaptation <adapt@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] uucpsend.c: use strchr() instead of BSD index(); drop malloc.h

`index()` is the BSD/legacy-POSIX alias of `strchr()` that was withdrawn in
POSIX.1-2008.  glibc still declares it through <strings.h>, but musl -- the
C library used by OpenHarmony/HarmonyOS -- only declares it when _BSD_SOURCE
is requested, which turns the five call sites into implicit declarations.
`strchr()` is ISO C, is already available through the <string.h> include at
the top of the file, and behaves identically here.

The same edit removes <malloc.h>: malloc()/free() are declared by <stdlib.h>
(already included) and <malloc.h> is a glibc/BSD extension header that musl
does not ship, so on OHOS the include would simply fail to resolve.

Upstream-Status: Submitted [portability]
Signed-off-by: HarmonyOS PC adaptation <adapt@example.invalid>
---
--- a/uucpsend.c
+++ b/uucpsend.c
@@ -23,7 +23,6 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
-#include <malloc.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
@@ -177,7 +176,7 @@
     tmpsite->next = NULL;
 
     /* Fill the structure, one by one */
-    if ((cp2 = index(cp, DELIMITER)) == NULL) {
+    if ((cp2 = strchr(cp, DELIMITER)) == NULL) {
       fprintf(stderr, "%s:%d invalid line.\n", fname, lcount);
       continue;
     }
